# Exhaustiveness: the summary block must be EXACTLY this. The branch adds one
# template to a single-app chart, so one app, one added resource, nothing
# modified - any extra app section or resource fails here rather than being
# quietly pinned by the next --regenerate.
expect:affected=1 changed=1 resources=+1,-0,~0
# THE PAIRING THIS CASE EXISTS FOR. The policy directory exists only on the
# branch, so the base side has none and is not linted, while the target side is.
# Both halves must be in the report together: the finding, and the note that
# explains why it is one-sided.
#
# Without the note, the report asserts something FALSE. Its label contract reads a
# [target]-only finding as "introduced by this change" - but this change
# introduced the POLICY, not the violation, and an unlinted base side is exactly
# how a pre-existing violation comes to look new. The note is the only thing in
# the report that says the two sides are not comparable for this linter.
must:\[base\] lint-kyverno#1 policies/kyverno-added: no policies on this side — not linted
must:\[target\] \[kyverno/require-pinned-images\] Deployment/cluster-info-web
# The note must be one-sided too, or it proves nothing: on target the policies ARE
# there and kyverno really ran, which is what makes the base-side note meaningful
# rather than a line argocdf prints for every side.
must-not:\[target\] lint-kyverno#1 policies/kyverno-added: no policies
# A side that was never linted cannot have findings. A [base] finding here would
# mean the skip did not happen and the case is testing nothing.
must-not:\[base\] \[kyverno/
# The violation must actually reach the render - a finding without it would mean
# kyverno linted something other than this diff.
must:^\+.*image: nginx:latest
